Drug-Induced Pancreatitis.

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient G.T.
* Age: 54 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 78 kg
* Medical History: Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (4 years), Hypertension, no history of gallstones, alcohol consumption, or hypertriglyceridemia.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Added Sitagliptin 100 mg orally once daily to existing Metformin therapy for inadequate glycemic control.
* Month 3 (Day 15): Patient developed sudden, severe epigastric abdominal pain radiating to the back, accompanied by persistent nausea and vomiting.
* Laboratory and Imaging Findings at Admission:
  * Serum Amylase: 840 U/L (Normal reference range: 30 to 110 U/L; elevated to greater than 3 times upper limit of normal)
  * Serum Lipase: 1,250 U/L (Normal reference range: 10 to 140 U/L)
  * Serum Triglycerides: 140 mg/dL (Normal reference range: under 150 mg/dL)
  * Abdominal CT Scan: Diffuse pancreatic enlargement, peripancreatic fluid collection, and absence of gallstones or biliary tree dilation.
* Intervention: Immediate permanent cessation of Sitagliptin; patient admitted to the inpatient ward for bowel rest, aggressive IV fluid hydration, and analgesic support.
* Follow-up (Day 10 post-discontinuation): Abdominal pain fully resolved; serum lipase and amylase returned to normal reference ranges; transitioned to Insulin therapy.

